    "case_id": "mxh045",
    "case_summary": "A 5-year-old female child is brought to a medical office with complaints of progressive abdominal pain that has intensified in the last 24 hours. The father reports a history of constipation, with no bowel movement for the past 72 hours and no bloody stools. She has experienced three episodes of vomiting since the previous night and is refusing food. Her medical and surgical history is unremarkable. Physical examination reveals upper abdominal distention with tympany, but no abdominal mass, involuntary guarding, or rebound tenderness is detected. What is the most probable underlying etiology of her clinical presentation?\n\nOptions:\nA. Hirschsprung disease\nB. Intestinal lymphoma\nC. Superior mesenteric artery syndrome\nD. Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction\nE. Volvulus\nF. Malrotation of the gut\nG. Pyloric stenosis\nH. Intussusception\nI. Meckel diverticulum\nJ. Duodenal web\n",
